WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.944
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.244 --> 00:00:08.166
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:18.154
Unit thirty-seven: Architecture and Space. In this unit, you will discuss how buildings shape the way we live.

4
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.952
sustainable architecture.

5
00:00:21.652 --> 00:00:27.704
sustainable architecture means design that reduces a building's environmental impact.

6
00:00:28.004 --> 00:00:32.348
Sustainable architecture uses local materials and natural light.

7
00:00:33.048 --> 00:00:37.117
Your turn. Say: sustainable architecture.

8
00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:41.561
sustainable architecture.

9
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.329
vernacular architecture.

10
00:00:45.029 --> 00:00:50.361
vernacular architecture means traditional buildings designed for local climate and culture.

11
00:00:50.661 --> 00:00:54.378
Khmer stilt houses are an example of vernacular architecture.

12
00:00:55.078 --> 00:00:59.449
Your turn. Say: vernacular architecture.

13
00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:03.847
vernacular architecture.

14
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.932
passive cooling.

15
00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:10.548
passive cooling means keeping buildings cool without air conditioning.

16
00:01:10.848 --> 00:01:14.542
High ceilings and shaded windows provide passive cooling.

17
00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:18.231
Your turn. Say: passive cooling.

18
00:01:20.931 --> 00:01:22.304
passive cooling.

19
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:24.527
adaptive reuse.

20
00:01:25.227 --> 00:01:29.432
adaptive reuse means giving an old building a new purpose.

21
00:01:29.732 --> 00:01:33.322
The old warehouse became a gallery through adaptive reuse.

22
00:01:34.022 --> 00:01:37.545
Your turn. Say: adaptive reuse.

23
00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:41.583
adaptive reuse.

24
00:01:42.583 --> 00:01:44.223
biophilic design.

25
00:01:44.923 --> 00:01:48.745
biophilic design means design that brings nature into buildings.

26
00:01:49.045 --> 00:01:52.844
Biophilic design includes plants, water and wood.

27
00:01:53.544 --> 00:01:56.975
Your turn. Say: biophilic design.

28
00:01:59.675 --> 00:02:01.233
biophilic design.

29
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:03.334
modernist.

30
00:02:04.034 --> 00:02:09.702
modernist means related to the 20th-century style focused on function and simplicity.

31
00:02:10.002 --> 00:02:13.441
The modernist building has clean lines and no decoration.

32
00:02:14.141 --> 00:02:16.852
Your turn. Say: modernist.

33
00:02:19.552 --> 00:02:20.483
modernist.

34
00:02:21.483 --> 00:02:22.508
brutalism.

35
00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:28.017
brutalism means an architectural style using raw, heavy concrete.

36
00:02:28.317 --> 00:02:32.917
Brutalism divides opinion: some love it, others hate it.

37
00:02:33.617 --> 00:02:36.792
Your turn. Say: brutalism.

38
00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:40.388
brutalism.

39
00:02:41.383 --> 00:02:42.918
public housing estate.

40
00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:48.590
public housing estate means a group of homes built by the government for rent.

41
00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.911
The public housing estate houses ten thousand families.

42
00:02:52.611 --> 00:02:56.576
Your turn. Say: public housing estate.

43
00:02:59.276 --> 00:03:00.753
public housing estate.

44
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.924
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:04.224 --> 00:03:07.965
Rithy, have you seen the buildings designed by Vann Molyvann?

46
00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:16.085
Yes! The National Olympic Stadium. It's a modernist building that makes clever use of passive cooling.

47
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:20.718
He drew heavily on vernacular architecture, didn't he?

48
00:03:22.017 --> 00:03:27.583
Exactly. Raised floors and wide roofs, just like traditional stilt houses.

49
00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:32.323
Many of his buildings have since been demolished, unfortunately.

50
00:03:33.617 --> 00:03:37.918
That's a real loss. Adaptive reuse would have breathed new life into them.

51
00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:45.779
Today, architects pay close attention to sustainable architecture again.

52
00:03:47.083 --> 00:03:52.360
And biophilic design. Our new library has trees growing inside!

53
00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.554
Grammar focus: Collocations in academic discussion.

54
00:03:57.854 --> 00:04:01.420
Academic collocations are fixed phrases that sound natural.

55
00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:08.074
Make clever use of, draw heavily on, pay close attention to, and breathe new life into.

56
00:04:08.374 --> 00:04:10.222
Learn them as complete chunks.

57
00:04:10.522 --> 00:04:16.620
Strong collocations make academic speech precise and natural; learn them as complete chunks.

58
00:04:17.617 --> 00:04:20.652
Now read and listen. Building for the Climate.

59
00:04:20.952 --> 00:04:25.471
Before air conditioning, builders had to work with the climate rather than against it.

60
00:04:26.783 --> 00:04:33.473
Vernacular architecture across Southeast Asia made clever use of shade, height and airflow.

61
00:04:34.783 --> 00:04:44.213
In the 1950s and 1960s, Cambodian architect Vann Molyvann combined these ideas with modernist design.

62
00:04:45.517 --> 00:04:51.696
His style, later called New Khmer Architecture, drew heavily on local traditions.

63
00:04:52.983 --> 00:04:57.932
Sadly, several of his buildings were demolished as Phnom Penh developed rapidly.

64
00:04:59.217 --> 00:05:06.243
Today, sustainable architecture is reviving interest in passive cooling and natural materials.

65
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:13.334
Adaptive reuse and biophilic design are also gaining popularity worldwide.

66
00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.809
The buildings of the future may look surprisingly like the wisdom of the past.

67
00:05:20.117 --> 00:05:22.723
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:23.023 --> 00:05:26.299
Keeping buildings cool without air conditioning is blank.

69
00:05:26.599 --> 00:05:31.623
A, passive cooling. B, brutalism. C, adaptive reuse.

70
00:05:35.617 --> 00:05:38.661
The answer is A: passive cooling.

71
00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.392
The design blank clever use of local materials.

72
00:05:43.692 --> 00:05:47.159
A, does. B, makes. C, takes.

73
00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.242
The answer is B: makes.

74
00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:57.376
The architect drew heavily blank tradition.

75
00:05:57.676 --> 00:06:00.680
A, on. B, in. C, at.

76
00:06:04.683 --> 00:06:07.182
The answer is A: on.

77
00:06:08.683 --> 00:06:11.449
Giving an old building a new purpose is blank.

78
00:06:11.749 --> 00:06:18.723
A, adaptive reuse. B, vernacular architecture. C, public housing estate.

79
00:06:22.717 --> 00:06:25.633
The answer is A: adaptive reuse.

80
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.473
According to the text, what did Vann Molyvann combine?

81
00:06:30.773 --> 00:06:40.023
A, vernacular ideas and modernist design. B, Greek and Roman styles. C, glass and steel only.

82
00:06:44.017 --> 00:06:47.990
The answer is A: vernacular ideas and modernist design.

83
00:06:49.483 --> 00:06:55.909
Now it's your turn. Part one: What should modern buildings in Cambodia learn from traditional architecture?

84
00:07:01.209 --> 00:07:07.504
Part two: Use two collocations, give two lessons, and describe one building you know.

85
00:07:13.804 --> 00:07:26.429
Modern buildings should make better use of shade and airflow, and draw on local materials. The Olympic Stadium, for example, pays close attention to ventilation and still feels cool on hot days.

86
00:07:26.729 --> 00:07:29.147
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.275
Great work today! You learned eight new words and collocations in academic discussion.

88
00:07:35.575 --> 00:07:40.108
Today's value is love of country. Love and take care of Cambodia.

89
00:07:40.408 --> 00:07:43.560
See you in Unit thirty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.907
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

